wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrical_equipment \"Electrical equipment\") |\n| Founded | 2016; 10 years ago (2016) |\n| Founder | Ralph Liebert [Edit this on Wikidata](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q5370909?uselang=en#P112 \"Edit this on Wikidata\") |\n| Headquarters | [Westerville, Ohio](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westerville,_Ohio \"Westerville, Ohio\"), U.S. |\n| Area served | Worldwide |\n| Key people | * [David M. Cote](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_M._Cote \"David M. Cote\") ([chairman](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chairman \"Chairman\")) * Giordano Albertazzi ([CEO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_executive_officer \"Chief executive officer\")) |\n| Products | Critical power systems, [uninterruptible power supply (UPS) systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uninterruptible_power_supply \"Uninterruptible power supply\"), [power distribution units (PDUs)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_distribution_unit \"Power distribution unit\"), [thermal management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermal_management_(electronics) \"Thermal management (electronics)\"), [data center infrastructure management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_center_infrastructure_management \"Data center infrastructure management\"), service and site operations |\n| Revenue | Increase US$10.2 billion (2025) |\n| [Operating income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earnings_before_interest_and_taxes \"Earnings before interest and taxes\") | Increase US$1.83 billion (2025) |\n| [Net income](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_income \"Net income\") | Increase US$1.33 billion (2025) |\n| [Total assets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asset \"Asset\") | Increase US$12.2 billion (2025) |\n| [Total equity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equity_(finance) \"Equity (finance)\") | Increase US$3.94 billion (2025) |\n| Number of employees | c. 34,000 (2025) |\n| Website | [vertiv.com](https://www.vertiv.com/) |\n| **Footnotes** [[1]](#cite_note-investors.vertiv.com-1)[[2]](#cite_note-2) |\n\n**Vertiv Holdings Co.** is an American provider of infrastructure and services for data centers, communication networks, and commercial and industrial environments. Headquartered in [Westerville, Ohio](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westerville,_Ohio \"Westerville, Ohio\"), Vertiv has around 34,000 employees worldwide, operating in more than 40 countries and with 30 manufacturing and assembly facilities.[[3]](#cite_note-:20-3)\n\nThe company has regional headquarters in: [Neuhausen am Rheinfall, Switzerland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuhausen_am_Rheinfall \"Neuhausen am Rheinfall\"); [Nanshan District, Shenzhen, China](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nanshan_District,_Shenzhen \"Nanshan District, Shenzhen\"); [Singapore](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singapore \"Singapore\"); and [Thane, Maharashtra India](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thane \"Thane\").\n\n## History\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=1 \"Edit section: History\")]\n\n### 1946–1965: Capitol Refrigeration Industries\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=2 \"Edit section: 1946–1965: Capitol Refrigeration Industries\")]\n\nVertiv began as Capitol Refrigeration Industries, established in 1946 by Ralph Liebert (1918-1984) in [Columbus, Ohio](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbus,_Ohio \"Columbus, Ohio\"). Liebert developed the first prototype precision air conditioner in his garage. In early 1965, the prototype was introduced to [IBM](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IBM \"IBM\") in [Chicago](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago \"Chicago\"). Recognising the machine's potential, IBM arranged for Liebert to debut his invention at the World Computer Conference in [Philadelphia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philadelphia \"Philadelphia\").[[4]](#cite_note-4)[[5]](#cite_note-5)\n\n### 1965–2000: Liebert Corporation\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: 1965–2000: Liebert Corporation\")]\n\nMain article: [Liebert Corporation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liebert_Corporation \"Liebert Corporation\")\n\n[Liebert Corporation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liebert_(company) \"Liebert (company)\") was formed in 1965 with five associates as the industry's first manufacturer of computer room air conditioning (CRAC) systems. Two years later, in 1967, Liebert made its first international move by establishing a Canadian distributor network. In 1968, the company moved into a new facility in [Worthington, Ohio](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worthington,_Ohio \"Worthington, Ohio\"). As its business continued to grow, the company broke ground once again and moved into a 150,000 square foot facility in Columbus, Ohio. In 1977, Liebert launched Conditioned Power Corporation to design and manufacture power distribution, conditioning and monitoring systems for the data processing industry.[[6]](#cite_note-:22-6)\n\nExpansion continued as Liebert broke ground for a second major manufacturing facility in [Delaware, Ohio](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaware,_Ohio \"Delaware, Ohio\"), in 1980. Ralph Liebert's son, Larry Liebert, took over the company as president in 1980 and served in this position until 1989. In 1981, Liebert became a public company, listed on the [NASDAQ](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nasdaq \"Nasdaq\") under the symbol LIEB. In 1983, Liebert acquired [Franklin Electric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franklin_Electric \"Franklin Electric\") subsidiary, Programmed Power Corporation, expanding the company's power division capabilities to include the design and manufacture of uninterruptible power supplies (UPS).[[6]](#cite_note-:22-6) That same year, Liebert opened its first overseas production facility in [Cork, Ireland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cork_(city) \"Cork (city)\").[[7]](#cite_note-:4-7) Liebert Corporation was acquired by [Emerson Electric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emerson_Electric \"Emerson Electric\") in March 1987,[[8]](#cite_note-8) remaining a wholly owned subsidiary of Emerson.\n\n### 2000–2016: Emerson Network Power\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: 2000–2016: Emerson Network Power\")]\n\nIn 2000, Emerson formed its Network Power (ENP) business unit, consolidating critical infrastructure technologies under a single brand. In 2001, the company increased its presence in Asia with the purchase of Avansys and formation of ENP India. ENP expanded its telecom industry solutions with the 2004 acquisition of Marconi outside plant and power system. In 2006, ENP acquired Germany-based Knürr, a provider of enclosure systems. In 2007, the [Energy Logic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_Logic \"Energy Logic\") energy efficiency model was developed and released.[[9]](#cite_note-9) [Avocent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avocent \"Avocent\") and [Chloride Group](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chloride_Group \"Chloride Group\") were acquired in 2009 and 2010 respectively.[[10]](#cite_note-10)\n\n### 2016–present: Vertiv\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=5 \"Edit section: 2016–present: Vertiv\")]\n\nIn December 2016, [Platinum Equity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Platinum_Equity \"Platinum Equity\") acquired the ENP business from [Emerson Electric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emerson_Electric \"Emerson Electric\") in a transaction valued in excess of $4 billion. The company was rebranded as Vertiv, and Emerson retained a subordinated equity stake.[[11]](#cite_note-11) Vertiv launched as a stand-alone business and appointed Rob Johnson as [chief executive officer](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_executive_officer \"Chief executive officer\").[[12]](#cite_note-12)[[13]](#cite_note-13)\n\nIn 2017, Vertiv sold its [ASCO](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASCO_Power_Technologies \"ASCO Power Technologies\") power switch business to [Schneider Electric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schneider_Electric \"Schneider Electric\") for $1.25 billion.[[14]](#cite_note-14) The company made three acquisitions in 2018: Energy Labs, Geist and MEMS. Through a merger with a [Goldman Sachs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldman_Sachs \"Goldman Sachs\")-led [special purpose acquisition company](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special-purpose_acquisition_company \"Special-purpose acquisition company\"), GS Acquisition Holdings, Vertiv became publicly traded on the [New York Stock Exchange](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Stock_Exchange \"New York Stock Exchange\") (NYSE: VRT) on February 10, 2020.[[15]](#cite_note-15)[[16]](#cite_note-16)\n\nIn 2021, Vertiv acquired E+I Engineering, a global provider of switchgear, busway and modular power solutions, for $1.8 billion.[[17]](#cite_note-17) In 2023, Vertiv's share price increased 252%, outperforming all companies in the [S&P 500 index](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S&P_500 \"S&P 500\").[[18]](#cite_note-18)\n\nIn March 2026, the company was added to the [S&P 500](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S&P_500 \"S&P 500\") under the Industrials GICS Sector.[[19]](#cite_note-19) As of 2026, the company is also part of the [Fortune 500](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fortune_500 \"Fortune 500\") list (No. 416)[[20]](#cite_note-20) and of the 2025 [Forbes Global 2000](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes_Global_2000 \"Forbes Global 2000\") (No. 1,174).[[21]](#cite_note-21)\n\n## Acquisitions\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=6 \"Edit section: Acquisitions\")]\n\nVertiv\n\n| Date | Company acquired | Country | Business | Reference |\n| September 2026 | King Environmental Services | [Republic of Ireland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Republic_of_Ireland \"Republic of Ireland\") Ireland | Fluid Management Services | [[22]](#cite_note-22) |\n| September 2026 | UtilityInnovation Group | [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") USA | Microgrid Solutions & Advanced Power Controls | [[23]](#cite_note-23) |\n| June 2026 | ThermoKey | [Italy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy \