ical Engineering & Testing | [[55]](#cite_note-55) |\n| 1990 | Control Concepts | [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") USA | Transient Voltage Surge Suppression (TVSS) | [[56]](#cite_note-56) |\n| 1988 | [ASTEC Power](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astec \"Astec\") | [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") USA | AC-DC & DC-DC Power Supplies | [[53]](#cite_note-:172-53) |\n| 1983 | Programmed Power Corporation | [United States](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States \"United States\") USA | Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS) | [[6]](#cite_note-:22-6) |\n\n## Markets\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=7 \"Edit section: Markets\")]\n\nVertiv's primary customers are businesses across three main end markets: [data centers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_center \"Data center\"), [communication networks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telecommunications_network \"Telecommunications network\") and commercial & industrial environments. Vertiv's principal offerings include:\n\n* Infrastructure & solutions: AC and DC power management, thermal management, and integrated modular solutions.\n* Integrated rack solutions: racks, single phase UPS, rack power distribution, rack thermal systems, configurable integrated solutions, hardware and software for managing IT equipment.\n* Services & spares: preventative maintenance, acceptance testing, engineering and consulting, performance assessments, remote monitoring, training, spare parts, and critical digital infrastructure software.[[57]](#cite_note-:0-57) Vertiv has over 320 service centers and employs more than 5,000 service field engineers globally.[[3]](#cite_note-:20-3)\n\nIn 2025, the company received 62% of its revenue in the [Americas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Americas \"Americas\"); 20% from [Asia–Pacific](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asia–Pacific \"Asia–Pacific\"); and 18% from [Europe, the Middle East and Africa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Europe,_the_Middle_East_and_Africa \"Europe, the Middle East and Africa\").[[3]](#cite_note-:20-3)\n\n### Manufacturing facilities\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=8 \"Edit section: Manufacturing facilities\")]\n\nVertiv has manufacturing facilities located in [Delaware, Ohio](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delaware,_Ohio \"Delaware, Ohio\"); [Ironton, Ohio](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ironton,_Ohio \"Ironton, Ohio\"); [Lincoln, Nebraska](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lincoln,_Nebraska \"Lincoln, Nebraska\"); [Anderson, South Carolina](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anderson,_South_Carolina \"Anderson, South Carolina\"); [Pelzer, South Carolina](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelzer,_South_Carolina \"Pelzer, South Carolina\"); [Tijuana](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tijuana \"Tijuana\"), [Mexicali](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexicali \"Mexicali\"), [Monterrey](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monterrey \"Monterrey\"), [Reynosa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reynosa \"Reynosa\"), [Letterkenny](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Letterkenny \"Letterkenny\"), [Burnfoot](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burnfoot,_County_Donegal \"Burnfoot, County Donegal\"), [Campsie](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campsey \"Campsey\"), [Bologna](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bologna \"Bologna\"), [Tognana](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piove_di_Sacco \"Piove di Sacco\"), [Volyne](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volyně \"Volyně\"), [Rugvica](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rugvica \"Rugvica\"), [Nové Mesto nad Váhom](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nové_Mesto_nad_Váhom \"Nové Mesto nad Váhom\"), [Ras Al Khaimah](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ras_Al_Khaimah \"Ras Al Khaimah\"), [Ambernath](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambarnath \"Ambarnath\"), [Chakan](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chakan,_Pune \"Chakan, Pune\"), [Pune](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pune \"Pune\"), [Mianyang](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mianyang \"Mianyang\"), [Jiangmen](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiangmen \"Jiangmen\") and [Johor](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johor \"Johor\").[[58]](#cite_note-58)\n\n### Research and development\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=9 \"Edit section: Research and development\")]\n\nVertiv invests approximately 5% of sales into research and development.[[59]](#cite_note-59) In 2025, Vertiv spent $441.7 million on research and development. Vertiv has approximately 3,000 registered [patents](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patent \"Patent\") and 1,900 registered [trademarks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trademark \"Trademark\").[[3]](#cite_note-:20-3)\n\n### Partnerships\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=10 \"Edit section: Partnerships\")]\n\nIn April 2021, Vertiv was announced as Founding Partner and Official Data Center Equipment Provider of [Columbus Crew](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbus_Crew \"Columbus Crew\").[[60]](#cite_note-60)\n\nIn May 2023, Vertiv was selected by [Nvidia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nvidia \"Nvidia\") after securing a $5m grant from [ARPA-E](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARPA-E \"ARPA-E\")'s COOLERCHIPS program. Their joint objective over the next three years is to address the challenge of cooling high-density compute by integrating direct liquid cooling and [immersion cooling](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immersion_cooling \"Immersion cooling\") into a single system. Vertiv was selected for its expertise in heat rejection technology.[[61]](#cite_note-61) In December 2023, Vertiv announced its collaboration with [Intel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intel \"Intel\") to provide pumped two-phase (P2P) liquid cooling infrastructure for the Gaudi3 AI accelerator.[[62]](#cite_note-62)\n\nIn March 2024, Vertiv joined the Nvidia Partner Network (NPN) as a Solution Advisor: Consultant partner.[[63]](#cite_note-63) In June 2024, Vertiv entered a strategic technology partnership with [Ballard Power Systems](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballard_Power_Systems \"Ballard Power Systems\") (NASDAQ: BLDP), a [proton exchange membrane](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proton-exchange_membrane \"Proton-exchange membrane\") (PEM) fuel cell company, to develop hydrogen fuel cell backup power solutions for data centers and critical infrastructure, scalable from 200 kW to multiple megawatts. The partnership targets zero-carbon and low-carbon alternatives to conventional diesel backup generators and covers deployments in North America and Europe, the Middle East and Africa (EMEA).[[64]](#cite_note-64)[[65]](#cite_note-65)\n\nIn October 2024, Vertiv co-developed with [Nvidia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nvidia \"Nvidia\") a complete 7-megawatt reference architecture for the Nvidia GB200 NVL72 platform, enabling customers to configure traditional data centers as AI factories. The reference design was released publicly at the OCP Summit.[[66]](#cite_note-66)[[67]](#cite_note-67)\n\nIn November 2024, Vertiv and [Compass Datacenters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compass_Datacenters \"Compass Datacenters\") announced a collaboration to develop a hybrid cooling solution capable of switching between air and liquid cooling to accommodate both conventional and high-density AI computing workloads. The initial units were scheduled for deployment at a Compass facility in the first quarter of 2025 as part of a multi-year supply arrangement.[[68]](#cite_note-68)\n\nIn July 2025, Vertiv and [Oklo Inc](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oklo_Inc. \"Oklo Inc.\"). (NYSE: OKLO), an advanced nuclear technology company, announced a collaboration to co-develop power and thermal management solutions for hyperscale and colocation data centers powered by steam and electricity from Oklo's [small modular reactor](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_modular_reactor \"Small modular reactor\") (SMR) nuclear power plants. A pilot technology demonstration was planned for the initial Oklo Aurora powerhouse at Idaho National Laboratory.[[69]](#cite_note-69)[[70]](#cite_note-70)[[71]](#cite_note-71)\n\nIn November 2025, Vertiv and [Caterpillar Inc](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caterpillar_Inc. \"Caterpillar Inc.\"). (NYSE: CAT) announced a strategic collaboration to integrate Vertiv's power distribution and cooling systems with Caterpillar's and its subsidiary Solar Turbines' power generation and combined cooling, heat and power (CCHP) capabilities. The arrangement was intended to produce pre-engineered reference architectures for AI data center deployments in power-constrained environments.[[72]](#cite_note-72)\n\nIn February 2026, Vertiv and Hut 8 Corp. (NASDAQ: HUT), an energy infrastructure company, announced a collaboration to integrate Vertiv's OneCore converged physical infrastructure into select Hut 8 data center projects, aimed at accelerating AI data center deployment schedules.[[73]](#cite_note-73) In March 2026, Vertiv and Generate Capital, PBC, an infrastructure investment platform, announced a collaboration to deliver integrated power and cooling solutions for data centers in the United States under a \"Bring Your Own Power & Cooling\" (BYOP&C) model.[[74]](#cite_note-74)\n\nAlso in March 2026, Vertiv announced its role in developing converged physical infrastructure designs for the Nvidia Vera Rubin DSX AI factory reference design, contributing simulation-ready digital power and cooling assets for Nvidia's data center design ecosystem. In June 2026, Vertiv introduced a production-grade digital twin for its SmartRun system integrated into the Nvidia Omniverse DSX Blueprint.[[75]](#cite_note-75)\n\nIn April 2026, Vertiv and CPower Energy, a virtual power plant (VPP) platform operator, announced a collaboration integrating Vertiv's EnergyCore Grid battery energy storage system with CPower's platform to enable data centers to participate in grid demand response programs and improve interconnection timelines.[[76]](#cite_note-76)\n\nIn June 2026, Vertiv signed a framework agreement with Edge-Serve, a Dubai-based data center developer; Integrated Roots Company Ltd. (IRCL), a Saudi Arabian construction contractor; and the Saudi Electricity Project Development Company (PDC), a subsidiary of Saudi Energy, to collaborate on data center infrastructure development across the [Gulf Cooperation Council](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulf_Cooperation_Council \"Gulf Cooperation Council\") (GCC) region, including the [United Arab Emirates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Arab_Emirates \"United Arab Emirates\") and [Saudi Arabia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saudi_Arabia \"Saudi Arabia\").[[77]](#cite_note-77)[[78]](#cite_note-78)\n\n### Competitors\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=11 \"Edit section: Competitors\")]\n\nVertiv's competitors include [Schneider Electric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schneider_Electric \"Schneider Electric\"), [Eaton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eaton_Corporation \"Eaton Corporation\"), [Legrand](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legrand_(company) \"Legrand (company)\"), [Huawei](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huawei \"Huawei\"), [Delta Electronics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Delta_Electronics \"Delta Electronics\"), Stulz, [Johnson Controls](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnson_Controls \"Johnson Controls\"), and Socomec.[[3]](#cite_note-:20-3)\n\n## Brands\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=12 \"Edit section: Brands\")]\n\nVertiv produces AC and DC [power management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Power_management \"Power management\") products, [switchgear](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Switchgear \"Switchgear\") and [busbar](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Busbar \"Busbar\") products, [thermal management](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermal_management_(electronics) \"Thermal management (electronics)\") products, integrated rack systems, and modular and management systems for monitoring and controlling digital infrastructure.[[79]](#cite_note-10K-79)\n\nVertiv's brands include:[[79]](#cite_note-10K-79)\n\n* Albér (Battery Monitoring)\n* [Avocent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avocent \"Avocent\") (IT Management)\n* E+I Engineering Group (Electrical Switchgear, Modular Power, Energy Management)\n* Energy Labs (Commercial and Industrial Thermal)\n* Geist (Rack PDU)\n* [Liebert](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liebert_Corporation \"Liebert Corporation\") (AC Power and Thermal)\n* NetSure (DC Power)\n* PowerBar (Busbar Trunking)\n\n## Leadership\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=13 \"Edit section: Leadership\")]\n\nGiordano Albertazzi became Vertiv's Chief Executive Officer on January 1, 2023,[[80]](#cite_note-80)[[81]](#cite_note-81) following the departure of former CEO Rob Johnson.\n\n## References\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Vertiv&action=edit&section=14 \"Edit section: References\")]\n\n1. [↑](#cite_ref-investors.vertiv.com_1-0) [\"Vertiv Reports Strong Fourth Quarter with Organic Orders Growth of 252% and Diluted EPS Growth of 200% (Adjusted Diluted EPS +37%)\"](https://investors.vertiv.com/news/news-details/2026/Vertiv-Reports-Strong-Fourth-Quarter-with-Organic-Orders-Growth-of-252-and-Diluted-EPS-Growth-of-200-Adjusted-Diluted-EPS-37/default.aspx). February 11, 2026. Retrieved February 21, 2026.\n2. [↑](#cite_ref-2) [\"The world depends on data we power and cool\"](https://www.vertiv.com/491e06/globalassets/documents/infographics/vertiv-fact-sheet-en-gl-2026.pdf) (PDF). *www.vertiv.com*. Retrieved February 21, 2026.`{{cite web}}`: CS1 maint: url-status ([link](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:CS1_maint:_url-status \"Category:CS1 maint: url-status\"))\n3. [1](#cite_ref-:20_3-0) [2](#cite_ref-:20_3-1) [3](#cite_ref-:20_3-2) [4](#cite_ref-:20_3-3) [5](#cite_ref-:20_3-4) [\"Vertiv Holdings Co Form 10-K 2025\"](https://d18rn0p25nwr6d.cloudfront.net/CIK-0001674101/74fd45d2-a423-47f5-8f9a-783c1ec9cfaa.pdf) (PDF). *Vertiv*. Retrieved February 21, 2026.\n4. [↑](#cite_ref-4) [\"Company will build air control equipment\"](http